@rem Exercises for lesson 01_intro (C track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise c-intro-theory-modulo
@type theory
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which arithmetic operator gives the remainder of an integer division? Write the operator symbol.
@answer
% | the % operator | modulo | modulus
@hint
It is not / and it is not *.
@explain
The % operator returns the remainder: 10 % 3 is 1. It works on integers only.

@exercise c-intro-choice-intdiv
@type choice
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does printf("%d", 7 / 2); print?
@choices
- 3.5
* 3
- 4
- 3.0
@explain
Both operands are int, so this is integer division and the fraction is thrown away: 7 / 2 is 3.

@exercise c-intro-fill-loop
@type fill
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Complete the loop so that it prints 0 1 2 3 4.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    for (int i = 0; ___; i++) {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
@answer
i < 5 | i <= 4 | i != 5
@match loose
@hint
The loop must stop once i reaches 5.
@explain
i < 5 is true for 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4, so five numbers are printed.

@exercise c-intro-predict-divmod
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 3;
    printf("%d %d\n", a / b, a % b);
    return 0;
}
@answer
3 1
@explain
10 / 3 is 3 (integer division) and 10 % 3 is the remainder 1.

@exercise c-intro-bug-scanf
@type bug
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This program should read an age and print it. Click the line that contains the bug, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age;
    printf("Enter age: ");
    scanf("%d", age);
    printf("You are %d\n", age);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
scanf("%d", &age); | scanf("%d",&age);
@match loose
@explain
scanf needs the address of the variable, so write &age. Without the & it treats the value of age as an address and crashes.

@exercise c-intro-theory-dowhile
@type theory
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which loop always runs its body at least once, because the condition is checked after the body?
@answer
do-while | do while | do...while | the do-while loop
@explain
do { ... } while (condition); runs the body first and tests the condition afterwards.

@exercise c-intro-theory-terminator
@type theory
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which character ends every C string? Write it the way it appears in code.
@answer
'\0' | \0 | the null terminator | null terminator | the null character | null character
@hint
Its value is 0.
@explain
A string is a char array that ends with '\0'. The array must be big enough for the characters plus this terminator.

@exercise c-intro-choice-array-size
@type choice
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Why do you pass the size of an array as a separate parameter when you pass the array to a function?
@choices
- Because the function receives a copy of the array and needs the size to copy it
* Because the function gets no size information about the array
- Because C does not allow arrays without a size
- Because sizeof does not exist inside functions
@explain
The function works on the caller's original array, but it only receives the address of the first element, so it cannot know how many elements there are.

@exercise c-intro-choice-assign
@type choice
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which line compares x with 5 instead of assigning 5 to x?
@choices
- if (x = 5)
* if (x == 5)
- if (x := 5)
- if (5 = x)
@explain
== compares, = assigns. if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true.

@exercise c-intro-predict-casts
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    printf("%d %.1f %.1f\n", 7 / 2, (float)7 / 2, (float)(7 / 2));
    return 0;
}
@answer
3 3.5 3.0
@explain
7 / 2 is integer division (3). (float)7 / 2 converts first, so it is 3.5. (float)(7 / 2) converts the already truncated 3, giving 3.0.

@exercise c-intro-predict-while
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int i = 1;
while (i <= 3) {
    printf("%d ", i * i);
    i++;
}
printf("\n");
@answer
1 4 9
@explain
The loop runs for i = 1, 2 and 3 and prints the square each time.

@exercise c-intro-predict-switch
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print? Watch the missing break.
@code
int n = 2;
switch (n) {
    case 1: printf("one ");
    case 2: printf("two ");
    case 3: printf("three "); break;
    default: printf("other ");
}
printf("\n");
@answer
two three
@explain
Without break execution falls through to the next case: case 2 prints "two ", falls into case 3, prints "three " and then breaks.

@exercise c-intro-predict-continue
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
for (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
    if (i % 3 == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    printf("%d ", i);
}
printf("\n");
@answer
1 2 4 5
@explain
continue skips the rest of the iteration, so the multiples of 3 (3 and 6) are not printed.

@exercise c-intro-predict-dowhile
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int n = 3;
do {
    printf("%d ", n);
    n--;
} while (n > 3);
printf("\n");
@answer
3
@explain
The body of a do-while runs once before the condition is checked; after that n is 2 and the condition n > 3 is false.

@exercise c-intro-predict-partial-array
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int a[5] = {1, 2, 3};
printf("%d %d %d\n", a[2], a[3], a[4]);
@answer
3 0 0
@explain
Partial initialisation fills the remaining elements with 0.

@exercise c-intro-predict-2d
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
int m[2][3] = {{1, 2, 3}, {4, 5, 6}};
int total = 0;
for (int r = 0; r < 2; r++) {
    total += m[r][1];
}
printf("%d\n", total);
@answer
7
@explain
m[r][1] is the middle element of each row: 2 and 5, which add up to 7.

@exercise c-intro-predict-string-length
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
What does this code print?
@code
char name[20] = "Alice";
int len = 0;
while (name[len] != '\0') {
    len++;
}
printf("%d %d\n", len, name[5]);
@answer
5 0
@explain
"Alice" has 5 characters; name[5] is the terminator '\0', whose value is 0.

@exercise c-intro-predict-by-value
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <stdio.h>

void tryToChange(int x) {
    x = 999;
}

int main() {
    int value = 5;
    tryToChange(value);
    printf("%d\n", value);
    return 0;
}
@answer
5
@explain
C passes ordinary variables by value: the function changes only its own copy.

@exercise c-intro-fill-average
@type fill
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
Complete the function so that it returns the average of the array as a double.
@code
double calculateAverage(int arr[], int size) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        sum += ___;
    }
    return (double)sum / ___;
}
@answer
arr[i]
size
@hint
Add every element, then divide by how many there are.
@explain
sum collects arr[i] for every index; casting sum to double before dividing avoids integer division.

@exercise c-intro-fill-const
@type fill
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Make MAX_SCORE a constant so that assigning to it later is a compile error.
@code
___ int MAX_SCORE = 100;
@answer
const
@explain
const makes a variable unchangeable.

@exercise c-intro-fill-scanf
@type fill
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Complete the call so that scanf stores the number in age.
@code
int age;
scanf("%d", ___);
@answer
&age
@explain
scanf needs the address of the variable, so you pass &age.

@exercise c-intro-bug-assign
@type bug
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
x is 3, but this program says "x is five". Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int x = 3;
    if (x = 5) {
        printf("x is five\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
@bugline 5
@answer
if (x == 5) {
@explain
if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and the condition is then always true. Comparing needs ==.

@exercise c-intro-bug-offbyone
@type bug
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
This program should print the sum of the five numbers, but it reads past the end of the array. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int numbers[5] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
        sum += numbers[i];
    }
    printf("Sum: %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
@explain
The valid indexes are 0 to 4. With i <= 5 the loop also reads numbers[5], which is out of bounds (undefined behavior).

@exercise c-intro-bug-missing-return
@type bug
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 2
@prompt
add should return the sum, but the result is garbage. Click the buggy line, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int add(int a, int b) {
    int c = a + b;
}

int main() {
    printf("%d\n", add(2, 3));
    return 0;
}
@bugline 4
@answer
return a + b; | return c;
@hint
What does add hand back to the caller?
@explain
add never returns a value. Reading the result of a non-void function that falls off its end is undefined behavior.
